  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence — External Remote Services", "technique": "T1133", "what_happened": "On 2027-09-14 at 03:14:08 UTC, Windows Security event 4624 fired on jumphost-prod-01 (agent.ip=10.42.11.18) with data.win.eventdata.logonType=10 (RemoteInteractive / RDP), data.win.eventdata.targetUserName='svc_admin', data.win.eventdata.ipAddress='185.220.101.45', authentication package Negotiate, Result=Success. The src IP 185.220.101.45 belongs to the 185.220.101.0/24 block which is a Tor exit relay range — a service account should NEVER authenticate from an anonymity network. The logon hour 03:14 UTC is also outside the operational baseline window for svc_admin (documented 08:00-19:00 UTC business hours).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 'Windows successful Remote Interactive logon from external IP' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1133 (External Remote Services). data.win.eventdata.targetUserName='svc_admin' is a SERVICE account, not a human user — service accounts have no interactive-logon use case, so logonType=10 is a strong signal regardless of the source IP.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T03:14:08+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Persistence — Valid Accounts", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "The exact same 4624 logonType=10 pattern (same targetUserName 'svc_admin', same data.win.eventdata.ipAddress 185.220.101.45, same destination jumphost-prod-01) repeated each subsequent day at ~03:14 UTC across 2027-09-15, 2027-09-16, 2027-09-17 and 2027-09-18 — five consecutive daily successful RDP logons from the same foreign Tor exit IP to the same internal jumphost. No malware artefact, no scheduled task, no autorun key, no abnormal child process — the persistence vector is the EXTERNAL DOOR itself (RDP exposed to 0.0.0.0/0 + valid credentials), not any endpoint backdoor.", "chain_reason": "Aggregator rule 'Repeated daily Remote Interactive logons from same external src to same internal dst within 7-day window' fired (rule.groups contains 'authentication_success' + 'external_remote_services'). The pattern (same src+dst+user+logonType=10, repeated daily, no endpoint persistence artefacts) is the canonical MITRE T1078 (Valid Accounts) signature WHEN combined with T1133 — credentials are abused to ride a legitimate remote-access protocol without dropping any malware.", "first_seen": "2027-09-15T03:14:11+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence — Internet-Exposed Admin Service", "technique": "T1133", "what_happened": "Pre-incident audit shows jumphost-prod-01 (10.42.11.18) has TCP/3389 (RDP) reachable from 0.0.0.0/0 (any source on the public Internet). Whether the firewall rule 'Allow-RDP-External' was added by an attacker post-compromise (which the PDF identifies as the canonical T1133 abuse pattern) or was a long-standing misconfiguration cannot yet be determined without the change-history audit — both possibilities lead to the same critical exposure. The combination INTERNET-FACING-RDP + SERVICE-ACCOUNT-LOGON-SUCCESS + TOR-EXIT-SOURCE-IP + DAILY-PATTERN = textbook External Remote Services persistence per Playbook 58.", "chain_reason": "data.win.eventdata.logonType=10 implies network-reachable RDP on data.dstport=3389. The 4624 success events from a Tor exit srcip outside any corporate VPN range prove the path was reachable from the public Internet, not from a VPN client behind perimeter. T1133 mapping is automatic.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T03:14:08+0000"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence — No Endpoint Malware", "technique": "T1133", "what_happened": "Sysmon, EDR and Wazuh File Integrity Monitoring on jumphost-prod-01 show ZERO suspicious artefacts during the 5-day window: no new scheduled tasks, no new services, no new Run/RunOnce keys, no new persistence-class file drops in C:\\\\Users\\\\svc_admin\\\\AppData or C:\\\\ProgramData. This is the defining property of T1133 (External Remote Services) per the PDF — persistence is achieved through the legitimate remote-access door, not through any endpoint implant. Endpoint-only detection would miss this entirely; the detection signal lives in authentication + perimeter telemetry, not in endpoint persistence telemetry.", "chain_reason": "Absence-of-endpoint-persistence-artefacts is itself the PDF Section D fingerprint of T1133 (ScheduledTasks=None, Services=None). It removes the AND-condition alternative explanations (no malware = not T1547 autorun, not T1053 scheduled task, not T1543 service persistence) and locks the persistence vector to T1133+T1078.", "first_seen": "2027-09-18T03:14:51+0000"}
  ],
